.\"
.\" WRITEV(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH WRITEV 2 "2026-09-23" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
writev \- write data from multiple buffers
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/uio.h>
.PP
.BI "ssize_t writev(int " fd ", const struct iovec *" iov ", int " iovcnt ");"
.fi
.PP
.nf
struct iovec {
    void   *iov_base;   /* start of a buffer */
    size_t  iov_len;    /* number of bytes to write from it */
};
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR writev ()
writes
.I iovcnt
buffers, described by the array
.IR iov ,
to the file referred to by the file descriptor
.IR fd .
The buffers are written in array order, each one completely before the
next, as if their contents had been concatenated and passed to a single
.BR write (2).
Buffers with an
.I iov_len
of zero are skipped.
.PP
On a datagram socket
.RB ( SOCK_DGRAM
or
.BR SOCK_RAW )
the buffers form
.B one
datagram: their concatenation is sent as a single message, exactly as
.BR sendmsg (2)
would send it.
If the buffers total zero bytes, one empty datagram is sent.
.PP
.BR writev ()
is a system call (number 526 in the native personality).
The Linux, FreeBSD and NetBSD personalities route their own
.BR writev ()
numbers to the same implementation.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, the number of bytes written is returned.
As with
.BR write (2),
this may be less than the total of the
.I iov_len
fields when the file cannot accept all of the data.
.PP
On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
If an error occurs after some bytes have been written, the number of
bytes written is returned instead.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
.I fd
is not a valid file descriptor or is not open for writing.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I iov
or one of the
.I iov_base
buffers is outside the accessible address space.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I iovcnt
is negative or greater than 1024.
.TP
.B EMSGSIZE
.I fd
is a datagram socket and the buffers total more than the largest
datagram the socket can send.
.TP
.B EDESTADDRREQ
.I fd
is a datagram socket that has not been connected.
.PP
Any error that
.BR write (2)
can return for
.I fd
may also be returned.
.SH CONFORMING TO
POSIX.1-2008.
.SH NOTES
Before Substrate 2026-09,
.BR writev ()
was implemented in the C library as a loop of
.BR write (2)
calls, one per buffer.
On a datagram socket that sent one datagram per buffer, splitting a
message built as a header plus a body.
.SH EXAMPLE
Send a two-part message as one UDP datagram on a connected socket:
.PP
.EX
#include <sys/uio.h>

struct iovec iov[2] = {
    { hdr,  sizeof hdr },
    { body, body_len   },
};
if (writev(sock, iov, 2) < 0)
    perror("writev");
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR write (2),
.BR sendmsg (2),
.BR readv (3),
.BR socket (2)
